  "lyrics": "[Verse]\nThe needle slides back to the very first mark\nThe rusted machine turns once more in the dark\nYou say it like prayer or a curse on the tongue\nTo fetch back the bells that were already rung\nThe shadow is stretching across the floorboards\nAs the dust makes a choir of the old broken chords\n\n[Verse]\nA circular road where the gravel is gray\nYou trade in tomorrow for one yesterday\nThe clock on the mantle is holding its breath\nLike a gambler who plays for a life or a death\nYou walk through the doorway you exited then\nJust to stand in the quiet and say it again\n\n[Chorus]\nOh the heavy wheel rolls on the soft river mud\nThere is iron and clover inside of the blood\nIt is built in the bone and it lives in the eye\nTo watch every summer eventually die\nBut you keep on reaching for what you have lost\nNo matter the winter and no matter the cost\n\n[Verse]\nThe prophets are sleeping on benches of stone\nWhile the king counts the seeds that he never has sown\nThe letter is signed but the ink is still wet\nWith the things that the memory cannot forget\nA circle is drawn where the straight line should run\nUnderneath the cold stare of a tired out sun\n\n[Verse]\nThe boots on the porch have a long way to go\nBut the path only leads to the valleys you know\nThere is nothing quite new in the way that we part\nIt is just the old hunger that gnaws at the heart\nThe mirror is clouded and cracked at the rim\nBut the ghost in the glass is still looking for him\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nThe rain on the roof has a familiar sound\nLike the turning of wheels on a hollowed out ground\nThe words are all spent but the feeling remains\nRunning like water through silver-lined veins\n\n[Chorus]\nOh the heavy wheel rolls on the soft river mud\nThere is iron and clover inside of the blood\nIt is built in the bone and it lives in the eye\nTo watch every summer eventually die\nBut you keep on reaching for what you have lost\nNo matter the winter and no matter the cost",
